<p>Nobody puts maintenance on a roadmap. It has no launch date, no demo, no applause. Yet almost everything I admire most has been maintained with the same care that went into making it.</p>

<p>There is a particular kind of intelligence in keeping a thing alive. You have to remember why old decisions were made. You have to resist the urge to rewrite everything just because you are bored. You have to fix the leak without falling in love with the plumbing.</p>

<h2>The long middle</h2>

<p>Launch is a moment. Maintenance is the long middle, where the real life of a product happens. It is the period when users stop being an audience and start being inhabitants. They notice small things. They build habits around them. They become attached to details you barely remember choosing.</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>Making something is an act of imagination. Keeping it good is an act of attention.</p>
</blockquote>

<p>I’ve started to schedule maintenance the way I schedule new work. Not as a cleanup task at the end, but as a first-class part of the week. An hour for the small fixes. A morning for the uncomfortable questions. A day, every few months, to look at the whole thing with fresh eyes.</p>

<p>It is not glamorous. It is the work that makes everything else possible.</p>
